R13 RUNTIME — A HOSTILE FILENAME AGAINST THE RUNNING ARTIFACT
audit host, 2026-09-04. Artifact under test: BUILD_ID _DzYjfCuvno7KJhZM8SWY
(HEAD 9389da3), api on 127.0.0.1:3052, Origin header = NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL.

Why this file exists. R13 had unit tests, mutation H, and the static harness
row G2 — but nothing had ever POSTed a hostile filename at a running server.
The brief forbids satisfying a behavioural guarantee with source scans alone,
so it was sent for real. Doing that found a defect the tests could not see.

  ===== R13 RUNTIME: a hostile filename on the FINAL-HEAD artifact 2026-09-04T01:38:24Z =====
  Claim under test: a filename carrying HTML, a traversal and a NUL-ish tail is
  accepted or refused WITHOUT its bytes reaching a path or a rendered name.
  Artifact: pid 9450, BUILD_ID _DzYjfCuvno7KJhZM8SWY. Fixture: /tmp/r30/dl-deploy.pdf
  --- filename sent (literal): ["><img src=x onerror=alert(1)>../../../etc/passwd;rm -rf ~.pdf]
  {"error":"Unsupported file extension. Allowed: .pdf."}
  --- http 400
  --- jobId: <none>
  
  --- the 400 above is a PROBE DEFECT: curl -F treats ';' in the value as a
      parameter separator, so the filename it actually sent was truncated at the
      semicolon and no longer ended in .pdf. Re-sent with the multipart body built
      by hand (python3), filename inserted verbatim.
  --- filename sent (literal): ["><img src=x onerror=alert(1)>../../../etc/passwd.pdf]
  500 {"error":"Unexpected server error while submitting the job."}
  --- filename sent (literal): [`id`$(whoami)&&rm -rf ~.pdf]
  202 {"jobId":"cmtmacm5h00b5uoaibu3orbr7"}
  --- filename sent (literal): [....//....//etc/passwd.pdf]
  202 {"jobId":"cmtmacm6v00b9uoaiye7kpsby"}
  
